      "problem": "Compute the gradient of the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14220728/assets/77705e38df3333765cc11467778e9b53cebe851f8a63733eacb2434e517e779f.svg) with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14220728/assets/0f887f6d96d27bc69edc756dcc8b572d17da7c22e15dcdae33f6450ddc9f52ea.svg)",
      "worked_solution": "Writing ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14220728/assets/7bc0b1ab99477802ba35c8a6ec27513de07dd4af1143edde3ea9c9cf8c660af6.svg), we have\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x})= \\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{x}) = 2A\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14220728/assets/f7ec32802bae881a1a7027998118d23d42104e2fdf3d586042c02fe65f536af1.svg)",
